    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.largest_common_subgraph - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.largest_common_subgraph.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.largest_common_subgraph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find the largest induced subgraphs that this graph and another have in\n    common.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.largest_common_subgraph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find the largest induced subgraphs that this graph and another have in\n    common.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.largest_common_subgraph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Find the largest induced subgraphs that this graph and another have in common.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind the largest induced subgraphs that this graph and another have in common.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L26618-L26762){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.largest_common_subgraph(\n    other,\n    symmetry=True,\n    node_attr=None,\n    edge_attr=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Largest common subgraph](../assets/graphs/largest-common-subgraph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: graph square, the 4-cycle a, b, c, d, beside graph paw, a triangle x, y, z with a pendant w on z. The biggest induced subgraph they share is a path of three nodes, found 8 ways. The first match is in the accent color in both graphs: x maps to b, z to a and w to d, so the path x, z, w lands on b, a, d. y stays faint; its path is a symmetric copy of x's, so it isn't listed again.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA common induced subgraph is a set of nodes of `other` whose induced subgraph (the\nnodes with every edge among them) also appears, as an induced subgraph, in this graph.\nThis method finds the largest such node sets and returns every way of matching them.\nComparing two molecules, two versions of a network, or a query against a database\ngraph by their largest shared structure gives a similarity that, unlike an exact\nsubgraph search, still says something when neither graph contains the other.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[other]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The `Graph` to compare with. It is not modified. Both graphs must have the same\n    directedness.\n\n<code>[symmetry]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (default), mappings that differ only by an automorphism of `other` are\n    reported once (see `subgraph_isomorphisms()`). If `False`, every mapping is listed.\n\n<code>[node_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | list[str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional node attribute name, or list of names, whose values must be equal for\n    matched nodes.\n\n<code>[edge_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | list[str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional edge attribute name, or list of names, whose values must be equal for\n    matched edges.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[dict[int, int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   One dictionary per largest common subgraph, mapping node IDs of `other` to node IDs\n    of this graph. All have the same size. If `other` has no nodes, the one empty\n    mapping; if this graph has none, an empty list.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If one graph is directed and the other is not.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThis is the largest common subgraph search of ISMAGS (Houbraken et al., 2014), as in\nNetworkX's `ISMAGS(G, other).largest_common_subgraph()`: `other` is first matched whole,\nas an induced subgraph, into this graph; if it doesn't occur, every subset with one node\nfewer is tried, and so on, until some subset occurs. With `symmetry=True`, the\nconstraints of `subgraph_isomorphisms(..., symmetry=True)` prune the matches, and a\nremoved node whose coset holds a larger node still in the subset is swapped for it, so\nsymmetric subsets are tried once. The mappings go from `other` to this graph, the\nreverse of NetworkX's graph-to-subgraph dictionaries. The problem is NP-hard and the\nnumber of subsets grows exponentially as the common part shrinks, so this suits small\ngraphs, or graphs that share most of their structure.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~subgraph_isomorphisms`) \n\n:   List the occurrences of a whole pattern.\n\n[](`~is_subgraph_isomorphic`) \n\n:   Check whether a whole pattern occurs.\n\n[](`~graph_edit_distance`) \n\n:   Measure how far apart two graphs are.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA square and a triangle with a pendant share a path of three nodes, and nothing larger:\n\n::: {#33a56a73 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nsquare = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"a\", \"b\"), (\"b\", \"c\"), (\"c\", \"d\"), (\"d\", \"a\")])\npaw = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"x\", \"y\"), (\"y\", \"z\"), (\"z\", \"x\"), (\"z\", \"w\")])\n\nfound = square.largest_common_subgraph(paw)\nlen(found)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n8\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe 8 matches are the square's 4 three-node paths, each run in both directions, laid\nonto the path `x`-`z`-`w` (the path from `y` is a symmetric copy, so it is skipped):\n\n::: {#14ee222f .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n{paw.get_label_from_id(k): square.get_label_from_id(v) for k, v in found[0].items()}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n{'x': 'b', 'z': 'a', 'w': 'd'}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### What caffeine shares with benzene\n\nBy shape alone, all of benzene fits in caffeine, as its ring of six atoms:\n\n::: {#683edd02 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmolecules = ec.molecules()\ncaffeine, benzene = molecules[\"caffeine\"], molecules[\"benzene\"]\n\nfound = caffeine.largest_common_subgraph(benzene)\nlen(found), sorted(caffeine.get_label_from_id(n) for n in found[0].values())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(1, ['C3', 'C4', 'C5', 'C6', 'N3', 'N4'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nMatching elements, the six carbons of benzene have to land on carbons. The most that\nfit is four, in seven ways. The largest common subgraph doesn't have to be connected:\nthe first match is the chain C3–C4–C5 plus C6, which is bonded to none of them, just as\nthe fourth carbon of benzene is bonded to none of the other three:\n\n::: {#57ba5b64 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfound = caffeine.largest_common_subgraph(benzene, node_attr=\"element\")\ncarbons = list(found[0].values())\nlen(found), [caffeine.get_label_from_id(n) for n in carbons]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n(7, ['C3', 'C4', 'C5', 'C6'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#a372af7f .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nbonds = [\n    (u, v) for _, u, v in caffeine.get_edges_as_tuples() if u in carbons and v in carbons\n]\n\ncaffeine.plot(\n    color_by=\"element\", highlight=carbons, highlight_edges=bonds, label_by=\"label\", seed=1\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast: